---
title: Sobel sequence under non-monotone opposing
description: |
  A concrete model exhibiting a two-step Sobel sequence for [+]->.

  We show:
    (1)  do(sd1) [+]-> q          [True]
    (2)  do({sd1,sb3}) [+]-> ~q   [True]

  This is only possible if the opposing relation is non-monotone with respect
  to group expansion — i.e., Def 3.4 is relaxed.

  Agents: sd (agent 1), ha (agent 2), sb (agent 3).
  Agent 1 always performs sd. Agents 2 and 3 independently choose to act or not.

  Opposing stipulations (non-standard — Def 3.4 NOT assumed):
    ha2 |> sd1   — ha2 opposes sd1 when acting alone
    sb3 |> sd1   — sb3 opposes sd1 when acting alone
    Neither ha2 nor sb3 opposes the group {sd1,sb3}.

  This violates Def 3.4 monotonicity: sb3 |> sd1 but sb3 ⊬ {sd1,sb3}.
  (An agent cannot oppose a group of which they are a member — this is the
  minimal non-monotonicity needed.)

  Witness sets:
    free_do(sd1)       = { h1 }          (sd1 acts; ha2, sb3 both absent)
    free_do({sd1,sb3}) = { h3, h4 }      (sd1+sb3 act; ha2 not an opposer of group)

  free_do({sd1,sb3}) ⊄ free_do(sd1) — monotonicity fails.

  Results:
    h1: q   → do(sd1) [+]-> q holds        (all free_do(sd1) histories give q)
    h3: ~q  → do({sd1,sb3}) [+]-> ~q holds (all free_do({sd1,sb3}) histories give ~q)
    h4: ~q

  Note: this model cannot be verified by the current evaluator, which
  implements standard Def 3.4 semantics. The opposing rules would need to
  be hand-specified per group rather than derived from individual opposings.
opposings:
  sd1: [ha2, sb3]
  "{sd1,sb3}": []
---
classDiagram
direction BT
  m --> m1 : h1({sd1, nop2, nop3})
  m --> m2 : h2({sd1, ha2,  nop3})
  m --> m3 : h3({sd1, nop2, sb3})
  m --> m4 : h4({sd1, ha2,  sb3})
  m1: q
  m2: q
  m3: ~q
  m4: ~q
